-- Slice 6 (proof caps), the PASS-THROUGH accept -- external code cannot MINT a
-- proof cap, but it CAN receive one as a parameter and pass it through
-- unchanged. Here `App` (a non-declaring module) declares
-- `relay(m : Cap(Db.Migrated)) : Cap(Db.Migrated) do m end`: the returned cap
-- is the exact one received as a parameter, so Check 6's declared-return-type
-- rule is satisfied (`Cap(Db.Migrated)` IS in the parameter caps). `App`
-- declares `needs Db.Migrated` because it uses `Cap(Db.Migrated)` in a
-- signature outside the declaring module (Check 1). This is the intended way a
-- proof cap flows through a system -- receive-and-pass-through -- and together
-- with the declaring module's `mint_cap` (see `accept/t62`) is one of the only
-- two ways to obtain a `Cap(P)`. See core-march-types.md §2.8.13.
mod Top do
  mod Db do
    proof cap Migrated
  end

  mod App do
    needs Db.Migrated

    fn relay(m : Cap(Db.Migrated)) : Cap(Db.Migrated) do m end
  end
end
